havior, before proposing fixes</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/systematic-debugging/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>test-driven-development</name>\n    <description>Use when implementing any feature or bugfix, before writing implementation code</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/test-driven-development/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>using-git-worktrees</name>\n    <description>Use when starting feature work that needs isolation from current workspace or before executing implementation plans - creates isolated git worktrees with smart directory selection and safety verification</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/using-git-worktrees/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>using-superpowers</name>\n    <description>Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring Skill tool invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/using-superpowers/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>verification-before-completion</name>\n    <description>Use when about to claim work is complete, fixed, or passing, before committing or creating PRs - requires running verification commands and confirming output before making any success claims; evidence before assertions always</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/verification-before-completion/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>writing-plans</name>\n    <description>Use when you have a spec or requirements for a multi-step task, before touching code</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/writing-plans/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>writing-skills</name>\n    <description>Use when creating new skills, editing existing skills, or verifying skills work before deployment</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/writing-skills/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>tdd</name>\n    <description>Test-driven development. Use when the user wants to build features or fix bugs test-first, mentions &quot;red-green-refactor&quot;, or wants integration tests.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/tdd/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>to-spec</name>\n    <description>Turn the current conversation into a spec and publish it to the project issue tracker: no interview, just synthesis of what you&apos;ve already discussed.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/to-spec/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>to-tickets</name>\n    <description>Break a plan, spec, or the current conversation into a set of tracer-bullet tickets, each declaring its blocking edges, published to the configured tracker (edges as text in one file per ticket locally, or native blocking links on a real tracker).</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/to-tickets/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>trycycle</name>\n    <description>Invoke trycycle only when the user requests it by name.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/trycycle/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>typeset</name>\n    <description>Improves typography by fixing font choices, hierarchy, sizing, weight, and readability so text feels intentional. Use when the user mentions fonts, type, readability, text hierarchy, sizing looks off, or wants more polished, intentional typography.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/typeset/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n</available_skills>\n</skills>","cwd":"<cwd>\n/Users/nick/code/roast.ts-rewrite-15-prompts\n</cwd>"},"timestamp":1795206409234,"toolsAdded":[{"name":"read","description":"Read the contents of a file. Supports text files and images (jpg, png, gif, webp, bmp). Images are sent as attachments. For text files, output is truncated to 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). Use offset/limit for large files. When you need the full file, continue with offset until complete.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to read (relative or absolute)"},"offset":{"type":"number","description":"Line number to start reading from (1-indexed)"},"limit":{"type":"number","description":"Maximum number of lines to read"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"bash","description":"Execute a bash command in the current working directory. Returns stdout and stderr. Output is truncated to last 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). If truncated, full output is saved to a temp file. Optionally provide a timeout in seconds.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["command"],"properties":{"command":{"type":"string","description":"Shell command to execute"},"timeout":{"type":"number","description":"Timeout in seconds (optional, no default timeout)"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"edit","description":"Edit a single file using exact text replacement. Every edits[].oldText must match a unique, non-overlapping region of the original file. If two changes affect the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead of emitting overlapping edits. Do not include large unchanged regions just to connect distant changes.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path","edits"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to edit (relative or absolute)"},"edits":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","required":["oldText","newText"],"properties":{"oldText":{"type":"string","description":"Exact text for one targeted replacement. It must be unique in the original file and must not overlap with any other edits[].oldText in the same call."},"newText":{"type":"string","description":"Replacement text for this targeted edit."}}},"description":"One or more targeted replacements. Each edit is matched against the original file, not incrementally. Do not include overlapping or nested edits. If two changes touch the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead."}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"write","description":"Write content to a file. Creates the file if it doesn't exist, overwrites if it does. Automatically creates parent directories.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path","content"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to write (relative or absolute)"},"content":{"type":"string","description":"Content to write to the file"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"live_context_annotate","description":"Create, resolve, or list branch-local continuity annotations. Create uses a current mirror block ID or active Pi entry ID. pin keeps a bounded exact textual source visible; continuity keeps a durable reason/next-action pointer; archive is recall-only.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["action"],"properties":{"action":{"type":"string","enum":["create","resolve","list"]},"source":{"type":"string","description":"Current CTX_TURN block ID or active-branch Pi entry ID"},"id":{"type":"string","description":"Annotation ID for resolve"},"title":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":120},"reason":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":400},"futureAction":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":400},"retention":{"type":"string","enum":["pin","continuity","archive"]},"resolution":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":400}}}},{"name":"live_context_recall","description":"Recall the exact textual source for a branch-local annotation by ID. Output is bounded to 128-8000 estimated tokens (default 2000) and validated against the durable Pi entry's content hash.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["id"],"properties":{"id":{"type":"string","description":"Durable live-context annotation ID"},"maxTokens":{"type":"integer","minimum":128,"maximum":8000}}}}]}}
{"type":"message_start","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<file name=\"/var/folders/wc/j7p9ktnx3d99v5xm95s9h2480000gn/T/redeye-1795206408869-t8to3itpamo.md\">\n---- SPEC CONTEXT ----\n# TypeScript rewrite of Roast\n\n**Status:** ready-for-agent\n\nThis is the index of a self-contained spec. The settled decisions are in\n`spec/00-decisions.md`; each area is specified in its own file under\n`spec/` (listed under \"Area files\"). Where this index and an area file\ndisagree, the \"Resolved open questions\" section below wins, then the area\nfile, then `spec/00-decisions.md`.\n\nArchitectural decisions: ADR-0003 (trust hash v2, partly supersedes\nADR-0001) and ADR-0004 (TypeScript package, raw fetch).\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nRoast is installed into a project with npm, but it is a Go binary. Every\nrelease builds six platform packages plus a launcher, runs a license\ncollection script per target, and needs a Go toolchain next to Node. The\nmaintainer works in TypeScript and wants one language, one toolchain, and\nthe TypeScript and npm conventions shared by their other packages. Users\nsee none of this directly, but they pay for it in release friction,\nplatform-package install quirks (`EBADPLATFORM`, forced workspaces), and a\nlauncher that can resolve the wrong `roast` binary.\n\n## Solution\n\nRewrite Roast in TypeScript as one pure-JS npm package, `2.0.0`, that\nbehaves like the Go version at commit `cd08f10`: same commands, flags,\ndefaults, exit codes, JSON reports, run records, and security model. The\nonly differences are the deliberate changes listed below. Runtime\nlibraries are bundled into a single ESM file, so installing Roast still\nadds no transitive packages to a user's project. Users upgrade by bumping\nthe dependency; the one visible step is approving the project config once\nmore (ADR-0003).\n\n## Implementation Decisions\n\n### Area files\n\n| File | Area |\n| ---- | ---- |\n| `spec/00-decisions.md` | Settled decisions and area-file conventions |\n| `spec/01-cli.md` | CLI surface, `review` orchestration, exit codes |\n| `spec/02-config.md` | Project config, built-in providers, roles |\n| `spec/03-trust-credentials.md` | Trust (hash v2), stored credentials |\n| `spec/04-scope.md` | Review scope: diffs, untracked, renames, caps |\n| `spec/05-context.md` | Finder context, navigation tools, grounding |\n| `spec/06-llm-protocols.md` | `chat`, `responses`, `anthropic` adapters |\n| `spec/07-agent-loop.md` | Agent loop, token ledger, wrap-up |\n| `spec/08-finder-prompts.md` | Finder stage, findings, fingerprints, prompts |\n| `spec/09-verify-render.md` | Verifier, report rendering, textsafe |\n| `spec/10-records.md` | Run records, bundles, `runs` / `runs show` |\n| `spec/11-snapshot.md` | Snapshots, `runs checkout` / `runs prune` |\n| `spec/12-gate.md` | Gate (pre-commit hook), `ignore` |\n| `spec/13-login.md` | `login`, `logout`, OpenRouter PKCE |\n| `spec/14-doctor-init-skill.md` | `doctor`, `init` starter, `skill` |\n| `spec/15-packaging.md` | Build, tests, lint, CI, release, M0, M6 |\n\n### Modules\n\nOne module directory per area under the source root, mirroring the Go\npackages: cli, config, trust, credentials, scope, context, llm (protocol\nadapters, SSE parser, agent loop, ledger), review (finder), prompts,\nverify, render, textsafe, record, runbundle, snapshot, gate, skill. Two\nshared modules sit below them:\n\n- **gocompat**: Go-compatibility helpers that parity depends on --\n  `isGoSpace` / `goTrimSpace` (Go `unicode.IsSpace`, which differs from JS\n  `\\s` and `trim`), `goExt` (Go `filepath.Ext`, which differs from Node\n  `path.extname` for names like `docs/.md`), `truncateUtf8`, and\n  `formatFixed2` (Go `%.2f`, which rounds exact ties to even unlike\n  `toFixed`), plus the Go-style duration parser.\n- **fsutil**: atomic file writes with explicit modes (trust record,\n  credentials, hook files, run records).\n\nThe CLI has one entry, bundled to one file. There is no library API.\n\n### Deliberate changes from the Go baseline\n\nProduct changes (from `spec/00-decisions.md`):\n\n1. No `completion` command.\n2. No Windows support (`os: darwin, linux`).\n3. Node `>=22.18`, checked at startup with a clear error.\n4. Trust hash v2 (ADR-0003); Go-era approvals are not honored.\n5. `DEBUG=roast:*` tracing, never including bodies, headers, prompts, or\n   credentials.\n6. Version `2.0.0`.\n\nTightenings and fixes (resolved open questions):\n\n7. JSON keys match exactly (case-sensitive) everywhere: `.roast.json`,\n   `credentials.json`, bundle manifests, model tool arguments. JSON `null`\n   still means \"absent\", as in Go.\n8. Every HTTP redirect is refused as an endpoint error: inference requests\n   and key validation alike.\n9. Provider error bodies are read at most 32 KiB.\n10. `grep_repo` forces `GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS=0` so the secret-file\n    denylist cannot be disabled from the environment.\n11. A run ID must be a plain name: `.`, `..`, and path separators are\n    rejected.\n12. `runs checkout` clears an inherited `GIT_INDEX_FILE`, and resolves a\n    relative target directory against the working directory once, used\n    consistently by every git call.\n13. Cached tokens are subtracted once, not twice, for `chat` and\n    `responses` usage.\n14. A `chat` stream chunk without usage keeps the last reported usage\n    instead of clearing it.\n15. Diff name lists and untracked-file lists use `-z`, and diffs run with\n    `core.quotePath=false`; non-ASCII paths are no longer skipped or\n    misattributed.\n16. The staged generated-file exclusion lists renames' deleted side too.\n17. The user's `diff.orderFile` is overridden so diff order is\n    deterministic.\n18. The size-fit breakdown uses a stable sort.\n19. A one-line hook file without a trailing newline gets one before\n    Roast's block is appended.\n20. `confidence_floor` values are validated to 0..1, matching the schema.\n21. Anthropic tool definitions carry the full JSON schema, including\n    `additionalProperties`.\n22. A second Ctrl-C force-exits with status 130. Ctrl-C at a raw-mode\n    secret prompt is cancellation (exit 5).\n23. Unreachable Go code is not ported: branch-survey scope, the `git_diff`\n    tool and the grounder's range corpus, and the test-only helpers\n    `DescribeCredential`, `IsLoopbackHost`, `DescribeEndpoint`.\n24. File names that are not valid UTF-8 are skipped with a warning.\n\n### Cross-cutting rules\n\n- **JSON encoding**: no HTML escaping of `<>&` or U+2028/U+2029. Parity is\n  defined on parsed JSON, not bytes. Tool results sent to models go\n  through one encoder in the llm area that sorts object keys, matching\n  Go's map ordering so prompts stay stable.\n- **Bytes and text**: git output is read as `Buffer`. Diff bytes stay\n  `Buffer` wherever they are hashed or recorded, so digests match Go.\n  Text is decoded with U+FFFD replacement only where it goes to a model or\n  a report. Lone surrogates from model output become U+FFFD when the llm\n  area decodes it; `textsafe` escapes any that remain as `\\uNNNN`. Config\n  validation rejects lone surrogates so the trust hash is always defined.\n- **Config numbers**: integer fields reject non-integer lexemes (`1.0`,\n  `1e3`) by checking raw token text through the JSONC parser's visitor.\n- **Output strings**: em dashes in Go's user- and model-facing strings\n  are kept, written as `\\u2014` escapes in TS source; docs and comments\n  never contain the character.\n- **CLI parsing**: yargs semantics are accepted where they differ from\n  cobra: decimal integers only, `--flag false` may be read as a value,\n  list flags split on plain commas. A parse failure uses the parsed\n  `--error-format` when available. EPIPE on stdout exits quietly with the\n  computed code.\n- **Process lifetime**: every tool that spawns a process kills it on\n  abort, and the CLI calls `process.exit` after flushing output.\n- **Runtime**: the `node:zlib` zstd ExperimentalWarning is suppressed (that\n  warning only). Tar archives support PAX path records when writing and\n  reading. Version, commit, and build date are injected at build time;\n  running from source reports `dev`.\n- **Network**: no proxy library is bundled. The README documents\n  `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1` and `NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS`; `doctor` warns when\n  `HTTPS_PROXY` is set but `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY` is not.\n\n### Milestones\n\nM0 scaffold; M1 config, trust, credentials; M2 scope and context; M3\n`chat` protocol, agent loop, finder, verifier, render, records -- first\nworking `npx roast review`; M4 `responses` and `anthropic`; M5 remaining\ncommands; M6 cutover (delete Go, platform packages, launcher; update\nAGENTS.md; release `2.0.0`). Details in `spec/15-packaging.md`.\n\n## Testing Decisions\n\n- A good test drives external behavior and asserts on exit codes, JSON\n  output, files written, and requests the fake server received. No\n  assertions on exact human-readable text, docs, or CSS.\n- **Primary seam**: the CLI run in-process (`run(argv, io)` with\n  injectable stdin, stdout, stderr, env, cwd) against a `node:http` fake\n  inference server speaking all three protocols, with a swappable `fetch`\n  so profiles on built-in endpoints reach the fake. Prior art: Go's\n  `executeContext` with `httptest`, `providerServer`,\n  `redirectDefaultTransport`, `settingsRepo`, `loginRepo`.\n- **Lower seams**, only where the CLI is too coarse: the token ledger,\n  trust hashing (frozen test vectors), `textsafe`, the SSE parser,\n  `gocompat`, and the agent loop driven by a scripted fake protocol\n  adapter.\n- **Swappable module functions** for terminal detection, browser opening,\n  and the browser-login timeout, as in Go.\n- **Gate**: a real `git commit` against a shell-stub `roast` at\n  `node_modules/.bin/roast`, as in Go's hook execution tests.\n- Tests that touch records, credentials, trust, or skills chdir into a\n  temporary Git repository and isolate `HOME`, `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`, and\n  `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`, as the Go tests do.\n- Go tests are ported area by area; each area file's test plan maps Go\n  test names to behaviors and seams.\n- A temporary differential harness builds the Go binary at `cd08f10` and\n  runs both CLIs against the same fake server, comparing exit codes,\n  parsed JSON, and run records, with an allowlist for the deliberate\n  changes above. It runs in CI on Ubuntu from M1 to M5 and is deleted at\n  M6.\n- The two rendered user prompts are checked against Go output by a\n  temporary differential test, deleted at M6.\n- Opt-in live tests (`ROAST_LIVE_*`) are kept and never run in CI.\n- The allocation-bound test for huge files becomes an RSS bound.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\n- Windows support.\n- A programmatic library API.\n- Behavior redesigns beyond the deliberate changes above; they come after\n  `2.0.0`.\n- Tracking or porting future upstream (`bluesky-social/roast`) changes.\n- Byte-identical compressed output (`.gz`, `.tar.zst`): decompressed bytes\n  must match, compressor output may differ.\n- Byte-identical help text with cobra.\n- Bundling a proxy agent.\n- Git submodules inside snapshots (documented as unsupported).\n\n## Further Notes\n\n- Kept as Go does, on purpose: `null` (not `[]`) for empty `notes`,\n  `matches`, and `lines`; `verified: true` with no findings; the monthly\n  index keeping the oldest sighting (the Go comment is wrong, not the\n  behavior); a global `core.hooksPath` affecting `gate disable`; `init`\n  writing to the working directory; doctor checking only user-level skill\n  installs; CLI hints saying bare `roast`; a malformed trust record\n  exiting 4; author routes discarding `--finder-*` overrides; an all-zero\n  finding from invalid `report_finding` JSON; index timestamps in local\n  time and `meta.json` in UTC; default adaptive thinking for\n  `claude-mythos-preview`.\n- The headless OpenRouter login (no `callback_url`) needs a manual check\n  against the live service before release.\n- `jsonc-parser` must run with trailing commas allowed: the `init`\n  starter writes one.\n- `@substrate-system/debug` and its dependencies must pass the license\n  allowlist in M0.\n- The uncommitted `package.json` lists `yargs`, `zod`, `jsonc-parser`\n  under `dependencies`; M0 moves them to `devDependencies`.\n\n\n---- TICKET ----\n15-prompts.md\n# 15: Prompts\n\n**Blocked by:** 03, 09\n**Touches:** `src/prompts/index.ts` (`finderSystem`, `verifierSystem`),\n`src/prompts/render.ts` (`renderFinderUser`, `renderVerifierUser`,\n`dataText`, `FinderUserVars`, `VerifierVars`), `src/review/classify.ts`\n(`classifyMode`, `classifyLangs`, `resolveLangs`), text-asset imports of\n`internal/prompts/templates/*.tmpl`, `src/assets.d.ts` (`*.tmpl` already\ndeclared by 01), `test/prompts/`, `test/review/classify.ts`,\n`test/diff/prompts.ts` (temporary), `test/index.ts`\n**Spec:** `spec/08-finder-prompts.md` 2.11-2.17, 3, 5 (\"Classification\",\n\"Prompts\"); `spec/15-packaging.md` 2.5 (text assets), 2.13 (Go baseline\nbuild); `spec.md` \"Testing Decisions\", \"Cross-cutting rules\" (output\nstrings)\n**Status:** ready-for-agent\n\n## What to build\n\nThe prompt layer of the finder and verifier, with no model calls.\n\nThe twelve baseline templates are imported as strings through the esbuild\ntext loader directly from `internal/prompts/templates/` (they move to\n`src/prompts/templates/` only at M6, ticket 37), so the Go embed and the\nTS bundle read the same bytes.\n\n`classifyMode` and `classifyLangs` use `goExt` from `gocompat` (ticket\n03), never `path.extname`, so dotfiles such as `docs/.md` classify like\nGo. `resolveLangs` implements the `--lang` rules of 2.12 and throws the\nCLI's `UsageError` (ticket 04); wiring it into `review` is ticket 20.\n\n`finderSystem(mode, scope, quick, langs, overridePath)` assembles the\nbase, scope framing, mixed addendum, language addenda, and quick addendum\nin the order of 2.13. `verifierSystem(overridePath)` returns the override\nor `verifier_system`. Override files are read verbatim (no trimming, no\nrendering, no defanging); a read failure is a plain `Error` with the 2.14\nprefixes. Callers pass paths already resolved through the config's\n`promptPath` (ticket 05); this module does not resolve them.\n\n`renderFinderUser` and `renderVerifierUser` are typed TS functions that\nreproduce the baseline `finder_user.tmpl` / `verifier_user.tmpl` output\nexactly (2.16, 2.17), with `dataText` defanging closing wrapper tags using\nRE2 semantics (ASCII `\\s`, Unicode simple case folding, ASCII word\nboundary) and `formatFixed2` from `gocompat` for confidence. U+2014\ncharacters in copied template lines are written as `\\u2014` escapes in\nthe TS source.\n\nTemporary differential test (deleted at M6 with the harness): under\n`test/diff/`, run by `npm run test:diff`, render both user prompts in Go\nand in TS from the same inputs and compare bytes. Reuse ticket 09's\nbaseline mechanism (worktree at `cd08f10`, `GOFLAGS` removed, cache under\n`node_modules/.cache/roast-diff/`) to build a small Go helper that calls\nthe baseline `prompts.FinderUser` / `prompts.VerifierUser` on JSON input;\nskip with a TAP skip when `go` is not on `PATH`, as the harness does.\nInputs must include a hostile case (closing tags in mixed case, `ſ`,\n`\\v`, `</claimx`), a survey case, and the 0.125-multiple confidence ties.\n\nThe finder stage (`find`, tool schemas, validation) is ticket 16.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Each of the twelve imported template strings equals the bytes of\n  its file under `internal/prompts/templates/` decoded as UTF-8.\n- [ ] `classifyMode` returns `code` for an empty list, `prose` when every\n  extension is prose, `mixed` when some are, `code` when none, and\n  compares extensions case-insensitively; `docs/.md` counts as prose.\n- [ ] `classifyLangs` maps, dedupes, and sorts tags and ignores unknown\n  extensions.\n- [ ] `resolveLangs` returns `[]` for prose without validating flags,\n  auto-classifies with no flag values, returns `[]` for a lone `none`,\n  throws `UsageError` for `none` combined with another pack and for an\n  unknown pack (case-sensitive, untrimmed), and otherwise keeps flag\n  order and duplicates.\n- [ ] `finderSystem` output contains the expected template assets in the\n  expected order for prose, code, and mixed modes in both `diff` and\n  `repo` scope (asserted by asset containment and index order, not\n  wording); prose diff mode has no diff framing.\n- [ ] The frontend addendum appears once for `react`, `frontend`, or\n  both; the hooks addendum only for `react`; the go addendum only for\n  `go`; `web` adds nothing; the quick addendum is always last.\n- [ ] An override replaces only the base, byte for byte, while scope,\n  mixed, language, and quick pieces still appear; a missing override\n  file rejects with an `Error` that is not a `UsageError`.\n- [ ] `verifierSystem` returns the override verbatim when given and the\n  `verifier_system` asset otherwise.\n- [ ] `renderFinderUser` survey output contains a `<manifest>` block and\n  no `<diff>` or `<files>` block; diff output contains `<diff>` and\n  `<files>` and no `<manifest>`.\n- [ ] `renderVerifierUser` output never contains the finder's reasoning\n  (the type has no such field) and, for untrusted text containing\n  closing tags, each wrapper tag closes exactly once and the final\n  instruction line is last.\n- [ ] `dataText` defangs `</CODE_QUOTE >` and a `ſ`-folded `</claim`,\n  and leaves `</\\vclaim` and `</claimx` unchanged.\n- [ ] The temporary differential test produces byte-identical\n  `finder_user` and `verifier_user` renderings from Go and TS for every\n  fixture input, and reports a skip (exit 0) when `go` is unavailable.\n- [ ] `npm run lint`, `npm run typecheck`, and the TS test suite pass,\n  and the Go build and Go tests still pass.\n\n\nThese are records of earlier runs. The ticket or spec may have changed since then; where they disagree, follow the ticket and spec. Do not repeat an approach these notes say failed unless you can see why it failed.\n\n---- PREVIOUS ATTEMPTS AT THIS TICKET ----\n## 2026-11-18 19:45 -- ts-rewrite/15 attempt 1 -- no commits\n\nAgent exit: 0. Checked items: none.\n\nThe agent left no notes.\n\n## 2026-11-20 12:05 -- ts-rewrite/15 attempt 1 -- no commits\n\nAgent exit: 0. Checked items: none.\n\nThe agent left no notes.\n\n## 2026-11-20 12:05 -- ts-rewrite/15 attempt 2 -- no commits\n\nEffort: high. Agent exit: 0. Checked items: none.\n\nThe agent left no notes.\n\n## 2026-11-20 12:26 -- ts-rewrite/15 attempt 1 -- timed out, then failed (tests)\n\nAgent exit: 1. Checked items: none.\n\nTest gate failed.\nLast 40 lines of `15-prompts.test.log`:\n```\nnpm warn using --force Recommended protections disabled.\nnpm notice run @substrate-system/roast@0.0.0 test\nnpm notice run npm run typecheck && npm run bundle && npm run build:test && npm run test:tap\nnpm warn using --force Recommended protections disabled.\nnpm notice run @substrate-system/roast@0.0.0 typecheck\nnpm notice run tsc -p tsconfig.json\nsrc/prompts/index.ts(168,43): error TS2345: Argument of type 'FinderUserVars' is not assignable to parameter of type 'Record<string, unknown>'.\n  Index signature for type 'string' is missing in type 'FinderUserVars'.\nsrc/prompts/index.ts(173,45): error TS2345: Argument of type 'VerifierVars' is not assignable to parameter of type 'Record<string, unknown>'.\n  Index signature for type 'string' is missing in type 'VerifierVars'.\nsrc/review/classify.ts(30,5): error TS1117: An object literal cannot have multiple properties with the same name.\nsrc/review/classify.ts(72,5): error TS1117: An object literal cannot have multiple properties with the same name.\nsrc/review/classify.ts(73,5): error TS1117: An object literal cannot have multiple properties with the same name.\nsrc/review/classify.ts(81,5): error TS1117: An object literal cannot have multiple properties with the same name.\nsrc/review/classify.ts(82,5): error TS11